  "body": "## Fix\n\nThe label release list shows nearly blank placeholders for legacy releases whose cover and artist photo are empty, even though the public Records catalog already contains their artwork. Use catalog artwork as a display fallback, matching platform identity or a unique normalized title plus credited artist. Preserve explicit covers and skip ambiguous matches.\n\nBroken images advance through the available sources before showing an accessible placeholder with readable theme colors. Failure state compares source values without serializing large uploaded data URLs into React keys, and equivalent source lists retain failures across rerenders. Populated but unparseable platform links also prevent title-based matching. The streaming poster uses the same artwork selection.\n\n## Validation\n\n- 13 focused regression tests pass: source priority, legacy credits, platform identity, malformed links, ambiguous versions, image failures, equivalent rerenders, and asynchronous catalog loading.\n- Browser verification on the local page loaded all six covers visible in the reported screenshot as real 300\u00d7300 images, using captured public API responses and the existing Spotify image URLs.\n- Isolated production build passed (TypeScript, Vite and initial-bundle budget); focused ESLint passed.\n- Cloudflare preview smoke loaded all six covers at 300\u00d7300 using a synthetic admin session and captured public API responses; image bytes came from the real Spotify URLs.\n- Added the two regression-fixture classifications required by the catalog authority audit; the complete local audit and hosted gate pass.\n\nAll hosted checks passed on final commit `760763642ae9641b19ef0adfb317f4243ff30a25`, including full UI quality, persona browser journeys, catalog audit, and Cloudflare/Vercel previews. The final Cloudflare preview also passed the six-cover smoke check.\n\nFrontend only; no API, migration, or catalog-data writes.\n\nPreview: https://fix-release-artwork-20260915.tdf-app.pages.dev/label/releases\n",
